<p class="lead">
  A sideways photo is almost always a metadata problem, not a content problem — the pixels are fine, the orientation flag just wasn't read correctly somewhere along the way. Fixing it takes a few seconds.
</p>

<h2>Rotate it in your browser (no upload, free)</h2>
<p>
  The <a href="/tools/rotate-image/">Rotate Image tool</a> on keptlocal rotates a photo by a preset angle or a custom degree, entirely on your device.
</p>
<ol>
  <li>Drop your image onto the tool.</li>
  <li>Click a preset — 90° left, 180°, or 90° right — or enter a custom angle.</li>
  <li>Download the result.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Why 90° rotations are lossless and custom angles aren't</h2>
<p>
  A digital image is a grid of pixels. Rotating exactly 90°, 180°, or 270° maps every pixel onto another exact grid position — the top-left pixel becomes the top-right pixel (for a 90° turn), and so on, with no ambiguity about where each pixel lands. Nothing needs to be invented or blended, so there's no quality loss.
</p>
<p>
  A custom angle — say, straightening a photo shot 3° off level — doesn't have this property. Rotating by 3° means most pixels no longer land on a clean grid position; the new image has to interpolate colors between the original pixels to fill in the rotated grid. This introduces a small amount of softening, more noticeable at larger custom angles and essentially invisible at the small corrections (a few degrees) most straightening actually requires.
</p>

<h2>When each preset applies</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>90° left / 90° right</strong> — the most common fix, for a photo that displays fully sideways (a portrait-oriented shot showing as landscape, or vice versa).</li>
  <li><strong>180°</strong> — for a photo that's completely upside down, which happens occasionally with certain scanning workflows or camera orientation bugs.</li>
  <li><strong>Custom angle</strong> — for straightening, not fixing gross orientation. A handheld shot with a slightly tilted horizon typically needs somewhere between 1° and 5° of correction.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Privacy: what happens to your photo</h2>
<p>
  Rotation happens on an HTML5 canvas in your browser — your photo is never uploaded. Open DevTools → Network while rotating and you'll see no outbound requests.
</p>

